    "evidence_text": "Silver sulfadiazine was associated with greater flexibility and ease of movement.\n\nAdverse events, including infection, were comparable between treatment groups.\n\nPatients with CGD also suffer from chronic inflammatory conditions, most prominently granuloma formation in hollow viscera.\n\nParents and a sibling were healthy heterozygous carriers.\n\nRNA-Seq analysis implicated downregulation of osteopontin as an important potential mechanism for the drug's action.\n\nInhibition of limb regeneration in the axolotl after treatment of the skin with actinomycin D.\n\nAfter an initial healing period, the control limbs regenerated normally.\n\nThe association between sterilizing activity and drug distribution into tuberculosis lesions\n\nThe finding that lesion penetration may contribute to treatment outcome has wide implications for TB.\n\nClinical features and treatment of peristomal pyoderma gangrenosum.\n\nOBJECTIVE To better characterize the clinical manifestations, diagnosis, and management of PPG.   \n\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURES Clinical and histopathologic features, associated disorders, and microbiologic findings.   \n\n\nFive patients had at least 1 relapse of PPG after initial healing.\n\nBoth patients whose stoma was relocated developed an ulcer at the new site.\n\nBiopsy of the skin lesion is not diagnostic but excludes other causes.\n\nTrauma to the skin of a predisposed patient may elicit the pustules or ulcerations associated with pathergy.\n\nJAMA.\n\n2000;284:1546-1548.\n\nSilver sulfadiazine for the treatment of partial-thickness burns and venous stasis ulcers.\n\nOutcomes included mortality, wound healing, speed of re-epithelialization, and infection rates.   \n\n\nRESULTS For burns, our search revealed 400 potential articles.\n\nNo human studies met the inclusion criteria.\n\nThese animal studies provided conflicting results.\n\nWhereas some support the use of SSD for treatment of partial-thickness burns, others question its effectiveness.\n\nPorphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) can occur in HIV patients.\n\nMoreover, chronic hemodialysis in renal failure may be a risk factor for PCT.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus infections.\n\nTreatment of these infections . . .",
